1
00:00:02,599 --> 00:00:04,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't need to be a specific theme, though.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:06,260
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole point is to, eh...

3
00:00:07,261 --> 00:00:18,424
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it was what you said a few days ago, when we spoke about how different people take different, eh... approach to practice, and then, how some people's approach involves forsaking clarity.

4
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:20,345
Hm.

5
00:00:21,986 --> 00:00:26,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you... if you're sort of craving for clarity of understanding, they would say they're just craving.

6
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:28,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... and by doing so, dismiss

7
00:00:31,924 --> 00:00:34,746
[SPEAKER_01]: There is absolutely no other method.

8
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:36,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is no method that you could prescribe

9
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:01,628
[SPEAKER_01]: They can then result automatically in clarity.

10
00:01:04,769 --> 00:01:11,991
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to arrive at it is to keep investigating and digging in, digging out for the meaning.

11
00:01:13,371 --> 00:01:21,813
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the suttas mean when they talk about dealt with a knife and then just keep digging, keep cutting, until you reach the clarity of understanding of certain universal truths.

12
00:01:24,913 --> 00:01:28,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we spoke about the idea of... well, how it was in the suttas, you know, when...

13
00:01:29,754 --> 00:01:43,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody declares, even if they don't necessarily declare their knowledge, if they have a level of understanding of dhamma, like, you know, if they meet with the monks, they'll be interrogating each other, not for the sake of argument, or for the sake of, um, oh, I know more than you do.

14
00:01:43,676 --> 00:01:51,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It was really just to test their own understanding and see if there is anything that they haven't considered, because of which they're maybe not getting the full picture.

15
00:01:51,598 --> 00:01:55,258
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no understanding that, that, uh, needs to, needs to be developed.

16
00:01:56,339 --> 00:01:58,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So the interrogation basically is done for the sake of clarity.

17
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:04,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Testing your own clarity, testing clarity of others, increasing your own clarity.

18
00:02:04,792 --> 00:02:11,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Even like, for example, even in cases like Arya Savakas, who already don't need anyone for their own clarity.

19
00:02:12,216 --> 00:02:19,540
[SPEAKER_01]: If they end up talking dhamma to others, it makes them think about dhamma, which means, makes them develop those principles more and more and more.

20
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:21,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a win-win, really.

21
00:02:22,541 --> 00:02:27,864
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh... the reason why you won't see that done today is because, well, it requires some degree of understanding to start with.

22
00:02:28,904 --> 00:02:34,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you just put together bouncing ideas of each other, and that cannot possibly result in the knowledge.

23
00:02:36,789 --> 00:02:38,069
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of ironic, isn't it?

24
00:02:38,129 --> 00:02:50,954
[SPEAKER_00]: People think, before they go to monasteries, they think that monasteries can be a place where people do investigate, where they have discussions together, and they go to the monastery, and then they sort of spend a bit of time, then they go to...

25
00:02:50,974 --> 00:02:52,114
[SPEAKER_00]: When do you have these meetings?

26
00:02:52,154 --> 00:02:53,174
[SPEAKER_00]: When you discuss things?

27
00:02:53,294 --> 00:03:03,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and then the meetings that are there are kind of a sutta study group, which is basically just a presentation of information, and then we all chip in with our opinion and review on a certain topic.

28
00:03:04,512 --> 00:03:06,913
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's really not like, okay, what does this mean?

29
00:03:07,733 --> 00:03:12,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if we encounter a contradiction, well, that must be resolved.

30
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:20,536
[SPEAKER_01]: But often, I remember, you know, people say, oh, well, I read this sutra and it means like this, like this, and then you would point out that there are other sutras that would contradict their understanding.

31
00:03:20,956 --> 00:03:26,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for the sake of, like, personally attacking somebody, but pointing out that that cannot be the universal principle.

32
00:03:38,739 --> 00:03:38,979
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

33
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:48,105
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's for the sake of your own clarity and your own understanding, you mustn't stop until there is at least sufficient level of that clarity and time.

34
00:03:49,066 --> 00:03:55,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you won't find contradictions later on, but certainly, if you're aware that they are, you must make the work to undo them.

35
00:03:57,491 --> 00:04:03,535
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is literally the most direct way you can have towards gaining the right view and understanding.

36
00:04:04,516 --> 00:04:07,138
[SPEAKER_01]: It's undoing your wrong views through self-questioning,

37
00:04:07,712 --> 00:04:11,713
[SPEAKER_01]: You're looking for the meaning of the universal phenomena in your experience.

38
00:04:17,114 --> 00:04:25,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I crossed my mind to, eh... well, we can be asking each other, eh... action, nature of action.

39
00:04:29,696 --> 00:04:37,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, obviously, we talk about it all the time, and it seems to be the convergence point for wholesome and unwholesome.

40
00:04:39,294 --> 00:04:41,776
[SPEAKER_01]: And there are obviously many, many views associated with it.

41
00:04:43,177 --> 00:04:46,599
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... people inevitably have to start with the wrong views.

42
00:04:47,679 --> 00:04:52,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But becoming aware of the wrong views as your starting point, helps you then make the effort towards undoing it.

43
00:04:53,904 --> 00:04:54,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not gonna...

44
00:04:55,284 --> 00:05:00,368
[SPEAKER_01]: There's an assumption that you'll find the right view, when you sort of go beyond your wrong views.

45
00:05:01,128 --> 00:05:01,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm...

46
00:05:01,588 --> 00:05:06,592
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm affected with the wrong views, so I just need to keep looking for the right answers, and then I'll get the right view.

47
00:05:06,632 --> 00:05:08,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view is basically undoing.

48
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:10,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Your views that are wrong.

49
00:05:11,503 --> 00:05:12,744
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you arrive at the right view.

50
00:05:13,124 --> 00:05:25,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's absolutely... it's inconceivable that those wrong views would be undone on its own, without the effort of actually unscrewing them, and making them fade away.

51
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:30,872
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the repetitive effort everybody talks about, and the gradual shelving of the ocean, and so on.

52
00:05:31,832 --> 00:05:37,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep applying that pressure and effort towards your own wrong views, and that's how you'll undo them.

53
00:05:38,428 --> 00:05:39,929
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're irreversible as well.

54
00:05:40,729 --> 00:05:42,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you undo them, you can't, like, reacquire them.

55
00:05:44,730 --> 00:05:56,394
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's this common notion of, oh, I just do these observances, methods, techniques, practices, and then I'll have a magical experience of the right view.

56
00:05:59,755 --> 00:06:01,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, interrogation, action.

57
00:06:06,798 --> 00:06:07,378
[SPEAKER_01]: What is, eh...

58
00:06:10,859 --> 00:06:13,480
[SPEAKER_01]: What is... where is action constituted?

59
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:18,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it in... say you're about to do something, physically.

60
00:06:21,882 --> 00:06:24,823
[SPEAKER_03]: Go for a walk, using your body for walking.

61
00:06:28,444 --> 00:06:30,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that action in your physical movements?

62
00:06:31,765 --> 00:06:37,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is that action in the decision to walk, even before you start walking?

63
00:06:42,073 --> 00:06:43,374
[SPEAKER_02]: And when does the action begin?

64
00:06:44,154 --> 00:06:45,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can say that.

65
00:06:51,999 --> 00:06:59,323
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the thing begins... You say a plant doesn't begin when it starts to grow outside the soil.

66
00:07:00,024 --> 00:07:01,084
[SPEAKER_02]: It begins at its roots.

67
00:07:02,005 --> 00:07:05,067
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, it begins there, where it's rooted.

68
00:07:05,267 --> 00:07:06,608
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where it grows from.

69
00:07:06,668 --> 00:07:08,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you intend to

70
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:18,725
[SPEAKER_02]: If you decide to walk outside, and then you start walking, that walking is secondary.

71
00:07:21,806 --> 00:07:23,607
[SPEAKER_01]: The walking is secondary to what?

72
00:07:23,627 --> 00:07:24,227
[SPEAKER_01]: To the decision.

73
00:07:24,247 --> 00:07:25,268
[SPEAKER_01]: To the decision to walk.

74
00:07:26,689 --> 00:07:34,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's secondary in terms of it's... it's more... it's more grown.

75
00:07:36,013 --> 00:07:37,334
[SPEAKER_02]: It proliferates.

76
00:07:37,814 --> 00:07:38,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it sort of starts...

77
00:07:39,395 --> 00:07:42,597
[SPEAKER_00]: It starts with...

78
00:07:42,657 --> 00:07:48,440
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I sort of see it as, like, there's a... basically, there's this possibility for going for a walk.

79
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:57,665
[SPEAKER_00]: There's this, like... even before a decision is made, there's this... that's a possibility.

80
00:07:57,865 --> 00:07:59,666
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what does start with that possibility?

81
00:08:01,247 --> 00:08:03,068
[SPEAKER_00]: You say, you see, it starts with that.

82
00:08:03,188 --> 00:08:04,669
[SPEAKER_00]: What... what starts with that?

83
00:08:04,709 --> 00:08:06,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Not... not starts, but, like... so...

84
00:08:08,335 --> 00:08:21,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, action is like... there are these possibilities and that one possibility of going for a walk presents itself.

85
00:08:24,083 --> 00:08:28,445
[SPEAKER_00]: But it may just be kind of bodily.

86
00:08:28,465 --> 00:08:29,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You may not even...

87
00:08:29,886 --> 00:08:31,006
[SPEAKER_00]: But is that an action?

88
00:08:31,687 --> 00:08:33,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that an action, then?

89
00:08:37,428 --> 00:08:43,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess not unless, yeah, if you don't, if you don't choose that possibility, then no.

90
00:08:44,472 --> 00:08:48,575
[SPEAKER_01]: If you choose that possibility, but also... Because that's, that's what the question is, was.

91
00:08:48,595 --> 00:08:49,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, what's action?

92
00:08:49,436 --> 00:08:51,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Where, where does the question, where does the action start?

93
00:08:53,618 --> 00:08:54,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, choice.

94
00:08:55,139 --> 00:08:58,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then there can be no action without choice.

95
00:08:58,901 --> 00:08:59,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Correct.

96
00:09:00,682 --> 00:09:01,823
[SPEAKER_01]: All of what you said is correct.

97
00:09:01,863 --> 00:09:02,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

98
00:09:02,463 --> 00:09:07,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You couldn't choose to act in a certain manner, if that's not given as a possibility.

99
00:09:08,106 --> 00:09:15,070
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're a wheelchair-bound, you can't use your legs, and the possibility of walking has been permanently removed.

100
00:09:15,170 --> 00:09:16,691
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's the important point.

101
00:09:16,891 --> 00:09:18,432
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not where the action starts.

102
00:09:20,482 --> 00:09:34,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes people, not necessarily differentiating that, ends up blaming possibilities and things that just arise in their mind on the level of possibilities, and they assume them to be the actions, and try to get rid of it all.

103
00:09:35,294 --> 00:09:45,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there is no... Action to walk, or rather choice to walk, is not the action.

104
00:09:46,943 --> 00:09:50,005
[SPEAKER_01]: The actual walking would be action of walking, which you maintain,

105
00:09:52,925 --> 00:09:54,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So, action without choice is inconceivable.

106
00:09:55,727 --> 00:09:57,909
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why we say every action is intention.

107
00:09:58,810 --> 00:10:05,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, intention to walk, the choice that you made, the intention that's present, hence you are walking while that choice is present.

108
00:10:06,595 --> 00:10:14,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another thing, how people will just jump into the assumption that the whole thing is linear.

109
00:10:16,137 --> 00:10:19,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you made a choice and that choice has ended when you started walking.

110
00:10:19,558 --> 00:10:20,838
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's absolutely not the case.

111
00:10:21,038 --> 00:10:22,398
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention remains there.

112
00:10:23,658 --> 00:10:26,699
[SPEAKER_01]: It gives the intelligibility to every single step you make.

113
00:10:26,819 --> 00:10:31,360
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't know what to do next if you had no knowledge of walking, no intention to walk.

114
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:35,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You would just set the foot forward and then, oh, what do I do now?

115
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:40,981
[SPEAKER_01]: But you already know that walking involves moving forward, using your limbs.

116
00:10:41,321 --> 00:10:45,162
[SPEAKER_00]: But people would also tend to think that the choice is

117
00:10:46,477 --> 00:10:49,619
[SPEAKER_00]: A kind of explicit, ah, now I'll do this.

118
00:10:49,639 --> 00:10:51,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's that's a topic in itself as well.

119
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:51,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

120
00:10:52,001 --> 00:10:52,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

121
00:10:52,461 --> 00:10:55,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, like, extra volitional or something like that.

122
00:10:55,283 --> 00:10:55,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

123
00:10:55,583 --> 00:11:07,950
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you, so, so, if you don't, but then there are occasions when you realize that you're doing something, and I didn't actually explicitly, in awareness, go, I'll stop, I'll do this.

124
00:11:07,990 --> 00:11:09,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then, actually, that's where I must have just missed it.

125
00:11:09,832 --> 00:11:11,993
[SPEAKER_00]: It must have been there, but I must have just missed this.

126
00:11:17,045 --> 00:11:18,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where does awareness of the choice come in, then?

127
00:11:18,945 --> 00:11:19,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

128
00:11:19,866 --> 00:11:21,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why the whole, like, wakefulness.

129
00:11:22,766 --> 00:11:29,428
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, the... what you said now, it's where people start with, in terms of their view.

130
00:11:31,188 --> 00:11:37,630
[SPEAKER_01]: They think, okay, so, only when I'm super extra mindful, that I made a choice, that's a mindful choice.

131
00:11:37,670 --> 00:11:37,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

132
00:11:38,905 --> 00:11:43,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything below that threshold of super-extra-mindfulness doesn't constitute a choice.

133
00:11:43,967 --> 00:11:53,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just must always remain equally mindful to this, like, overloading extent of I'm moving, I'm moving, I'm touching, I want to drink, I'm drinking, I'm sitting.

134
00:11:54,131 --> 00:11:59,634
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I, if I, if I find myself doing something and I didn't actually remember... Means I lost my mind for a second.

135
00:11:59,654 --> 00:12:00,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

136
00:12:00,374 --> 00:12:01,014
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done something wrong.

137
00:12:01,054 --> 00:12:01,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

138
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:05,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's unsustainable.

139
00:12:05,421 --> 00:12:06,322
[SPEAKER_01]: That kind of practice.

140
00:12:06,382 --> 00:12:10,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, incidentally, kind of the contemporary take on mindfulness.

141
00:12:10,525 --> 00:12:21,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, therefore, basically, we are constantly making choices without actually being aware at the time of doing it.

142
00:12:21,572 --> 00:12:23,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there are sub...

143
00:12:24,070 --> 00:12:26,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so that's the point that I was going to make.

144
00:12:26,352 --> 00:12:29,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the key to practice mindfulness.

145
00:12:30,477 --> 00:12:34,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not to tailor your choices to your assumed degree of mindfulness.

146
00:12:34,881 --> 00:12:40,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It's to refine your mindfulness to actually discern the choices that are already there and being made.

147
00:12:41,267 --> 00:12:42,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's completely the opposite direction.

148
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:46,333
[SPEAKER_01]: But that takes much more work.

149
00:12:47,212 --> 00:13:04,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Refining your discernment of the choices that they're enduring, because you're doing all these actions, as opposed to just sweep all that away, and now only what counts is what I decision, full-on, mindful presence of my every moment, what I decide, what I intend.

150
00:13:04,877 --> 00:13:06,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you always have to lapse from that kind of state.

151
00:13:07,918 --> 00:13:15,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead of trying to fit your choices into mindfulness, you refine your mindfulness to start seeing all these choices that are there on their own.

152
00:13:17,935 --> 00:13:19,756
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you also get to a prudence as well.

153
00:13:20,436 --> 00:13:31,881
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why there's... and the confusion is basically because it's hard to understand that if you're unaware of a choice, it doesn't mean it was unintentional.

154
00:13:32,341 --> 00:13:35,703
[SPEAKER_01]: It means simply your mind is so far removed from your self-awareness.

155
00:13:37,675 --> 00:13:40,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why then people say, okay, so I must make only intentional choices that count.

156
00:13:41,177 --> 00:13:52,401
[SPEAKER_01]: But you start with that, but you actually want to refine your mindfulness then further and further to see other, slightly less intentional, but still on the level of, on the threshold of intention, choices that you're making.

157
00:13:56,383 --> 00:14:02,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you then, for example, you, how would you then, for example, practice mindfulness practically?

158
00:14:03,586 --> 00:14:05,047
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you, how would you be mindful?

159
00:14:07,448 --> 00:14:18,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, no right or wrong answer.

160
00:14:19,232 --> 00:14:24,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Describe how you understand it.

161
00:14:24,494 --> 00:14:31,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, whatever understanding a person has, even if they don't have the fundamental understanding, it's a form of understanding for them.

162
00:14:31,916 --> 00:14:34,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the practice, then, is to refine that understanding.

163
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:40,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Get freed from contradictions or mistakes, and then take it further in that direction.

164
00:14:40,261 --> 00:14:42,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, that's wrong, here is the right answer.

165
00:14:44,802 --> 00:14:47,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, there's no wrong answer, really.

166
00:14:48,083 --> 00:14:49,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just accurate and less accurate.

167
00:14:52,284 --> 00:14:55,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to go about, during the day, practice mindfulness.

168
00:14:55,964 --> 00:14:56,544
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you do?

169
00:14:58,565 --> 00:15:01,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I come and ask you, how can I be mindful for... throughout the day?

170
00:15:01,826 --> 00:15:03,506
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you, eh... advise me?

171
00:15:04,770 --> 00:15:06,671
[SPEAKER_01]: What can I do?

172
00:15:09,073 --> 00:15:19,940
[SPEAKER_03]: Paying attention to the things that are already sort of happening.

173
00:15:20,701 --> 00:15:21,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Such as?

174
00:15:21,101 --> 00:15:21,521
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

175
00:15:21,601 --> 00:15:22,342
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't understand that.

176
00:15:22,842 --> 00:15:26,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Like right now, I'm sitting.

177
00:15:26,705 --> 00:15:29,887
[SPEAKER_03]: Like you said, I don't have to always keep thinking, I'm sitting, I'm sitting, I'm sitting.

178
00:15:30,847 --> 00:15:32,669
[SPEAKER_03]: But I do intend to sit.

179
00:15:40,518 --> 00:15:47,281
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's a... there's a degree of awareness, even if I'm not fully attending to that I'm sitting.

180
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:51,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how is that then... what do you mean by paying attention to that?

181
00:15:51,662 --> 00:15:54,963
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you... what do you say, paying attention to that?

182
00:15:56,284 --> 00:15:57,064
[SPEAKER_01]: What... what... what...

