369
00:31:30,732 --> 00:31:31,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

370
00:31:31,553 --> 00:31:41,975
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to not... Yeah, no, I just feel like, if, again, saying what I asked Aruna, if I want to learn, how would you tell me practically how can I do that?

371
00:31:42,015 --> 00:31:42,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

372
00:31:43,416 --> 00:31:44,316
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, what is the theme?

373
00:31:47,094 --> 00:32:14,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're basically saying is, if you don't forget the motive behind your own presently enduring action, you are dividing the weight or shifting some weight back at the origin of

374
00:32:15,488 --> 00:32:17,749
[SPEAKER_01]: What you're attending in front of you.

375
00:32:17,789 --> 00:32:20,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't it just the process of asking yourself, why am I doing this?

376
00:32:20,810 --> 00:32:22,131
[SPEAKER_00]: You would start with that, yeah.

377
00:32:22,511 --> 00:32:23,632
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I doing this?

378
00:32:23,732 --> 00:32:25,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Just asking that question.

379
00:32:25,032 --> 00:32:29,214
[SPEAKER_01]: A recognition of why you're doing it and what you're doing right now.

380
00:32:29,334 --> 00:32:32,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, I'm sitting here and talking or like I'm going for a walk.

381
00:32:32,595 --> 00:32:36,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, everything, sure, everything will be funneled into the most general purpose.

382
00:32:36,937 --> 00:32:38,618
[SPEAKER_01]: I am a monk, I will be free from suffering.

383
00:32:39,238 --> 00:32:42,840
[SPEAKER_01]: But that might be a bit too far removed for somebody when they just start.

384
00:32:44,003 --> 00:32:50,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to find slightly less general basis for your act.

385
00:32:50,406 --> 00:32:53,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I going for this walk?

386
00:32:53,927 --> 00:32:58,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I haven't moved my body for three days.

387
00:32:58,369 --> 00:32:59,710
[SPEAKER_01]: I was sick, so I need to move.

388
00:33:00,350 --> 00:33:01,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's your motivation.

389
00:33:02,571 --> 00:33:04,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you say, is there anything wrong with that motivation?

390
00:33:05,232 --> 00:33:05,412
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

391
00:33:06,593 --> 00:33:07,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can go for a walk.

392
00:33:07,953 --> 00:33:10,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do I go for a walk and so on?

393
00:33:10,274 --> 00:33:11,955
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another thing you should be considering then.

394
00:33:12,937 --> 00:33:20,841
[SPEAKER_01]: If exactly, sometimes, oh, because I'm kind of bored and restless in my kuti, like, oh, so I am acting out of unwholesome basis of restlessness.

395
00:33:22,282 --> 00:33:28,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, simply questioning yourself about the motive.

396
00:33:28,905 --> 00:33:35,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is your action that you're about to do, or you have been doing, if you caught yourself at the later stage, where is it rooted?

397
00:33:35,989 --> 00:33:40,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all you need to do in order to clarify the situation.

398
00:33:40,613 --> 00:33:44,754
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to every time arrive at the answer that because you want to be free from suffering and you're a monk.

399
00:33:45,194 --> 00:33:46,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, that's going to become implied.

400
00:33:47,435 --> 00:33:51,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but... Because see, like now, oh, I want to avoid acting out of ill-will.

401
00:33:51,336 --> 00:33:51,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

402
00:33:51,796 --> 00:33:58,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because from the other point of view, that's unwholesome and that prevents the fulfillment of my goal of being a monk and being free from suffering.

403
00:33:59,059 --> 00:34:00,759
[SPEAKER_01]: But on a practical basis, yeah.

404
00:34:00,779 --> 00:34:02,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I doing what I'm doing right now?

405
00:34:02,900 --> 00:34:03,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because of this.

406
00:34:04,600 --> 00:34:10,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply maintaining the knowledge of why you're doing whatever you're doing, that's your mindfulness.

407
00:34:10,768 --> 00:34:13,629
[SPEAKER_01]: That is basically beginning and the ending of mindfulness.

408
00:34:13,889 --> 00:34:18,830
[SPEAKER_02]: But you have to maintain your knowledge, but you have to question.

409
00:34:19,530 --> 00:34:21,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you question your motives, question your motives.

410
00:34:21,991 --> 00:34:23,111
[SPEAKER_02]: That generates the knowledge.

411
00:34:23,311 --> 00:34:24,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

412
00:34:24,172 --> 00:34:31,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just thinking, is there a danger that some people, when you ask yourself, why am I doing this?

413
00:34:31,774 --> 00:34:33,094
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a danger to psychologize.

414
00:34:33,114 --> 00:34:34,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean, yeah.

415
00:34:34,574 --> 00:34:36,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even, you just come up with some

416
00:34:38,526 --> 00:34:42,869
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep insisting you have to find motivation.

417
00:34:42,909 --> 00:34:47,492
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about finding the answer, necessarily, inasmuch as it's finding the motivation, which is then the answer.

418
00:34:47,612 --> 00:34:48,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing this?

419
00:34:48,793 --> 00:34:49,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

420
00:35:00,677 --> 00:35:01,918
[SPEAKER_01]: My parents never loved me.

421
00:35:01,938 --> 00:35:02,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

422
00:35:02,858 --> 00:35:04,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's not a motivation.

423
00:35:04,499 --> 00:35:05,840
[SPEAKER_01]: That's you chose that.

424
00:35:06,220 --> 00:35:08,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did you choose that to be a reason?

425
00:35:08,361 --> 00:35:11,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Because other people have parents that don't love them and they don't do the same.

426
00:35:11,742 --> 00:35:13,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did you make these choices?

427
00:35:15,284 --> 00:35:16,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

428
00:35:16,645 --> 00:35:27,310
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a danger of psychologizing in as much as there is a danger, if you don't do it at all, of completely ignoring it and just following the mechanical, methodical practice of a robotic technique.

429
00:35:28,938 --> 00:35:32,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, even if you start psychologizing, you ask yourself that.

430
00:35:32,660 --> 00:35:35,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, why am I now providing all these explanations for this?

431
00:35:35,402 --> 00:35:47,129
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's asking why and... but also taking responsibility for... Well, discerning the responsibility that's there, that you can't have no... have no choice but to have.

432
00:35:47,609 --> 00:35:51,371
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh...

433
00:35:51,391 --> 00:35:56,414
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, having the knowledge of the motivation

434
00:35:59,183 --> 00:35:59,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Attentions.

435
00:36:00,523 --> 00:36:01,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Behind things you attend, yeah.

436
00:36:04,205 --> 00:36:06,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Knowledge of the roots of your attention.

437
00:36:06,885 --> 00:36:07,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

438
00:36:07,526 --> 00:36:20,950
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that also then means, brings us back to all the talks we've been doing before about peripheral awareness and so on, means you can't attend, on the same level, the knowledge because of which you're attending all these personal, particular things.

439
00:36:23,371 --> 00:36:27,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you can't, or rather, if you do start attending to that knowledge,

440
00:36:29,733 --> 00:36:36,978
[SPEAKER_01]: As if it is an object of your attention, means then you're motivated by something else.

441
00:36:37,038 --> 00:36:42,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to discern motivation without needing to attend to it at the expense of everything else.

442
00:36:44,104 --> 00:36:46,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why mindfulness will result in knowledge.

443
00:36:46,906 --> 00:36:47,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what knowledge is.

444
00:36:49,147 --> 00:36:53,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping things in the back of your mind that you already know without having to think about them all the time.

445
00:36:55,352 --> 00:36:56,593
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what knowledge is.

446
00:36:57,263 --> 00:37:00,926
[SPEAKER_01]: When you don't know something, you have to think and attend and try to figure it out.

447
00:37:01,046 --> 00:37:06,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you do, automatically, it recedes into the background and becomes the significance of your experience, whatever you want to call it.

448
00:37:07,251 --> 00:37:12,715
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, you don't have to be going around thinking that 2 and 2 is 4, 2 and 2 is 4, 2 and 2 is 4, because you already know.

449
00:37:13,355 --> 00:37:13,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know what?

450
00:37:14,176 --> 00:37:15,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You know how to add things up.

451
00:37:16,358 --> 00:37:18,139
[SPEAKER_01]: You understood the principle of it.

452
00:37:18,539 --> 00:37:21,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not thinking about it.

453
00:37:21,902 --> 00:37:25,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the point here, to understand the principles of, eh...

454
00:37:26,393 --> 00:37:26,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca.

455
00:37:27,233 --> 00:37:29,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand the principle of dukkha.

456
00:37:33,536 --> 00:37:35,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it all revolves around the sense of self.

457
00:37:36,277 --> 00:37:40,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, having non-mindfulness of these things involves the self-view.

458
00:37:40,918 --> 00:37:44,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Having mindfulness of these things uproots the self-view, because it makes it redundant.

459
00:37:45,620 --> 00:37:51,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So you mean, like, so somebody who... someone who can drive, doesn't have to think about driving.

460
00:37:52,901 --> 00:37:53,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

461
00:37:53,582 --> 00:37:58,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and they can't think about the driving, see?

462
00:37:58,606 --> 00:37:59,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they don't know how to drive.

463
00:38:00,107 --> 00:38:04,910
[SPEAKER_01]: They can think about how to move the stick, how to twist the wheel, how to press the pedal.

464
00:38:05,511 --> 00:38:12,356
[SPEAKER_01]: When they understand, as in develop more general understanding of these particular actions, that's when they know how to drive.

465
00:38:12,696 --> 00:38:14,257
[SPEAKER_01]: And then all they think about is driving.

466
00:38:14,277 --> 00:38:16,739
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't think about every particular movement of the hand.

467
00:38:17,740 --> 00:38:21,443
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, it's like people... when people... people act normally like that.

468
00:38:22,330 --> 00:38:30,593
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they get into practice and then they try to undo this and become focused on every little thing you do, at the expense of what you're doing.

469
00:38:31,293 --> 00:38:33,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what you were led to believe, that that's what meditation is.

470
00:38:34,714 --> 00:38:36,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Moment to moment experience.

471
00:38:36,654 --> 00:38:39,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring the bigger picture, which is already a moment experience.

472
00:38:40,856 --> 00:38:43,997
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying, like before, I remember, you know, the one-pointedness.

473
00:38:44,537 --> 00:38:46,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's how people, like, refer to samādhi.

474
00:38:48,018 --> 00:38:48,438
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is...

475
00:38:50,686 --> 00:38:51,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Two ways you can go about it.

476
00:38:51,927 --> 00:38:58,272
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be one-pointedness, in terms of you focusing on one point so hard, that you forget about everything else.

477
00:38:58,292 --> 00:38:58,872
[SPEAKER_00]: You become absorbed.

478
00:38:59,213 --> 00:38:59,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

479
00:39:00,113 --> 00:39:04,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you discern one point that includes all these other things within it.

480
00:39:05,178 --> 00:39:06,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which one does it sound better?

481
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:13,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Focusing hard on one little instance, so I simply have no recognition of any other instances.

482
00:39:13,324 --> 00:39:17,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, finding the fundamental instance that already includes all of these instances.

483
00:39:18,092 --> 00:39:30,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And given that the Buddha gave so many similes... examples and similes of a gatekeeper, you don't go chase around every individual instance of a person in the city, you stand at the gate, you know they're inside, that's all you need to know.

484
00:39:30,538 --> 00:39:32,419
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a knowledge that all these people are in.

485
00:39:33,160 --> 00:39:33,480
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

486
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:34,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That's samādhi.

487
00:39:34,440 --> 00:39:35,181
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the practice.

488
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:44,946
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's harder to do, because you have to question yourself, and that will bring anxiety, that will bring doubt, that will be... and that's exactly why the five hindrances then become such a thing.

489
00:39:45,246 --> 00:39:47,367
[SPEAKER_01]: It's manifested through... through proper practice.

490
00:39:49,450 --> 00:39:57,415
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I just tell you, you know, just focus on your nostrils, focus on touching with soles of your feet, as if walking means I want to touch things with my soles.

491
00:39:57,535 --> 00:39:57,695
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

492
00:39:58,355 --> 00:40:00,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Walking means I want to move, I want to walk.

493
00:40:01,798 --> 00:40:13,725
[SPEAKER_01]: All these other particular actions you can look at, will be seen correctly only if you don't forget the general context of your intention to walk, that persists for the duration of your act of walking.

494
00:40:14,285 --> 00:40:16,727
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's that principle of the whole and the part.

495
00:40:16,767 --> 00:40:18,748
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only understand what part is,

496
00:40:19,690 --> 00:40:23,513
[SPEAKER_00]: From the context of the whole, you can't understand the part, you know.

497
00:40:23,973 --> 00:40:27,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like the part, you understand the part, then you put together to get the whole.

498
00:40:27,635 --> 00:40:28,356
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

499
00:40:28,376 --> 00:40:30,818
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to start with the whole to understand what the part is.

500
00:40:30,898 --> 00:40:33,900
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if sometimes you don't know what the whole is,

501
00:40:35,046 --> 00:40:40,587
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you're assembling parts to find the hole, means you only have a recognition that there must be a hole to these parts.

502
00:40:41,767 --> 00:40:49,829
[SPEAKER_01]: But what people hope is that there's absolutely zero knowledge of a hole, and somehow I'll magically arrive at the knowledge of a hole based on these random parts I'm doing.

503
00:40:53,270 --> 00:41:02,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, yes, being instructed, hearing the dhamma, as in getting the sense of the context that must be developed, does give the direction to all those individual actions.

504
00:41:02,171 --> 00:41:04,592
[SPEAKER_01]: But how individual those actions will become,

505
00:41:05,320 --> 00:41:07,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that depends how wrong your views are.

506
00:41:08,582 --> 00:41:13,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't say, oh, well, becoming a monk and wearing robes and eating once a day.

507
00:41:13,125 --> 00:41:17,388
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are individual actions towards your practice of freedom from suffering.

508
00:41:17,989 --> 00:41:25,734
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're not on the same level as individual actions of touching, touching, moving, moving, sitting, sitting, feeling, thinking, thinking, speaking, speaking, sleeping, sleeping.

509
00:41:28,096 --> 00:41:29,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are also individual actions.

510
00:41:29,257 --> 00:41:31,258
[SPEAKER_01]: But you see how much more they're misguided.

511
00:41:31,738 --> 00:41:32,439
[SPEAKER_01]: They are misguided.

512
00:41:34,095 --> 00:41:37,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the more you commit to it, the further away from the context you drift.

513
00:41:38,197 --> 00:41:40,119
[SPEAKER_01]: And exactly that can happen to monks as well.

514
00:41:40,139 --> 00:41:44,541
[SPEAKER_01]: When being a monk becomes about having my perfect robe, perfect stitch.

515
00:41:45,042 --> 00:41:46,743
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm such a beautiful disciple.

516
00:41:46,783 --> 00:41:51,886
[SPEAKER_01]: I always put Ajahn's cloth perfectly and I walk so softly, I speak so softly.

517
00:41:53,567 --> 00:41:56,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm a layman still, mentally, basically.

518
00:41:57,349 --> 00:41:57,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

519
00:41:57,956 --> 00:41:58,456
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

520
00:41:58,756 --> 00:42:04,499
[SPEAKER_01]: All these are individual actions, but it's whether you know that you're supposed to look for the context behind them or not.

521
00:42:04,759 --> 00:42:05,199
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

522
00:42:05,219 --> 00:42:14,603
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rather... That's when you become like a career monk, when it's about appearance, it's about this, it's about serving and do this, and not about understanding the context of your actions, the context of the suffering.

523
00:42:14,764 --> 00:42:16,684
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of easier to obsess over the...

524
00:42:17,005 --> 00:42:17,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

525
00:42:17,505 --> 00:42:19,826
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Sīla Bhārata Paramārtha is the first fetter.

526
00:42:21,226 --> 00:42:22,507
[SPEAKER_01]: People derogatory sort of...

527
00:42:23,268 --> 00:42:32,610
[SPEAKER_01]: derogatorily translated as as rites and rituals, because, you know, nobody's silly enough to worship rites and rituals as a as a vehicle to enlightenment.

528
00:42:33,010 --> 00:42:33,431
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

529
00:42:33,471 --> 00:42:35,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīlavāta paramāsa means virtue and duty.

530
00:42:37,932 --> 00:42:44,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, me doing the virtue, doing the duty, without understanding the context of it, means I'm fettered by things I'm doing.

531
00:42:44,313 --> 00:42:44,653
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

532
00:42:45,334 --> 00:42:51,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, imagine you take that even further, and I start doing all these mindless meditation techniques and everything else.

533
00:42:51,887 --> 00:42:57,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And, literally, you basically completely cement any access to the direction where the context of your actions are.

534
00:43:00,255 --> 00:43:07,801
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your context, your motivation, will govern your actions.

535
00:43:08,162 --> 00:43:08,382
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

536
00:43:08,502 --> 00:43:10,344
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you protect...

537
00:43:10,404 --> 00:43:10,804
[SPEAKER_02]: It always does.

538
00:43:10,824 --> 00:43:16,850
[SPEAKER_02]: ...that higher mind, that higher motivation, that big context, with your life,

539
00:43:18,301 --> 00:43:20,122
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to worry about much else.

540
00:43:20,162 --> 00:43:20,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

541
00:43:20,522 --> 00:43:20,982
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's like...

542
00:43:21,082 --> 00:43:21,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Absolutely.

543
00:43:21,803 --> 00:43:26,145
[SPEAKER_02]: ...that you get... you have to learn how to drive before you can get there.

544
00:43:26,165 --> 00:43:27,865
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is... what is it...

545
00:43:27,885 --> 00:43:29,846
[SPEAKER_02]: I want to develop kindness, loving kindness.

546
00:43:30,266 --> 00:43:30,847
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I must get...

547
00:43:30,927 --> 00:43:37,990
[SPEAKER_02]: I must get used to what kind things are, pick up the theme of kindness, and then hold... and then make that...

548
00:43:38,250 --> 00:43:39,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Develop the theme.

549
00:43:39,110 --> 00:43:46,654
[SPEAKER_02]: ...as the thing that I'm... my... my shining light in my life is that context, that... that sign of kindness.

550
00:43:49,385 --> 00:43:50,706
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I concentrate on.

551
00:43:51,226 --> 00:43:55,368
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I compose my life in, all around.

552
00:43:56,248 --> 00:44:02,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Without needing to sort of go through, like, daily repetition of particular actions, of expressing kindness to everyone...

553
00:44:02,632 --> 00:44:04,913
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't do... ...smiling and doing all these things.

554
00:44:04,953 --> 00:44:05,113
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

555
00:44:05,333 --> 00:44:06,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Kindness means absence of your will.

556
00:44:06,774 --> 00:44:08,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you will be naturally doing things.

557
00:44:08,735 --> 00:44:08,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

558
00:44:10,556 --> 00:44:11,536
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's where they're rooted.

559
00:44:11,576 --> 00:44:11,796
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

560
00:44:11,896 --> 00:44:18,940
[SPEAKER_02]: But if that's the theme of your... of the game, is kindness, then whatever I come into contact with, which I...

561
00:44:19,625 --> 00:44:26,231
[SPEAKER_02]: I will come into contact with things, because I'm eating, I'm going to the toilet, I'm speaking with people, so I'm getting sick.

562
00:44:27,291 --> 00:44:41,443
[SPEAKER_01]: But if... it doesn't matter what things you come across and against, if a backdrop against those things is the theme that you invested effort in developing, the theme of kindness, no ill will can seep into these particular actions, because they're at a lower level.

563
00:44:42,364 --> 00:44:43,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, pretty fine.

564
00:44:44,265 --> 00:44:46,067
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are unclear about the theme of kindness,

565
00:44:47,637 --> 00:44:49,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Lack of clarity will then give rise to what?

566
00:44:49,698 --> 00:44:52,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust, aversion, delusion, ignorance.

567
00:44:52,239 --> 00:44:54,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Always gives rise to these things.

568
00:44:54,880 --> 00:44:57,521
[SPEAKER_02]: A painful thing comes up, like an annoying thing.

569
00:44:59,061 --> 00:45:02,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it will test your clarity of the theme of kindness.

570
00:45:02,682 --> 00:45:04,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, someone starts to insult me.

571
00:45:04,723 --> 00:45:04,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

572
00:45:07,204 --> 00:45:07,604
[SPEAKER_02]: But that...

573
00:45:08,464 --> 00:45:15,887
[SPEAKER_01]: If you still recognize that it doesn't matter how powerful somebody is trying to push through the gate, all I need to do is stay at the gate.

574
00:45:16,541 --> 00:45:19,804
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to fight them where they are, or chase them inside.

575
00:45:19,904 --> 00:45:20,905
[SPEAKER_01]: I just need to stay at the gate.

576
00:45:20,925 --> 00:45:28,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just need to stay at the level of the theme of kindness, and you will then... And, again, that can only be developed if you keep doing it.

577
00:45:28,351 --> 00:45:30,873
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep practicing it, then you develop confidence in it.

578
00:45:31,273 --> 00:45:32,094
[SPEAKER_02]: But, that's the thing.

579
00:45:32,154 --> 00:45:32,955
[SPEAKER_02]: If someone...

580
00:45:33,535 --> 00:45:41,522
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you protect that mind of kindness, which now you've been maintaining for a while, someone comes to insult you,

581
00:45:43,217 --> 00:45:45,378
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to worry about what your response will be.

582
00:45:45,458 --> 00:45:45,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

583
00:45:45,698 --> 00:45:49,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it will come from that place of where you are always situated.

584
00:45:49,779 --> 00:45:50,720
[SPEAKER_00]: If that mind is clear.

585
00:45:50,880 --> 00:45:53,240
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've not lost sight of that mind of kindness.

586
00:45:53,501 --> 00:45:58,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And... And losing sight, just to add, means muddling the knowledge.

587
00:46:00,063 --> 00:46:06,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not losing... because sometimes people will say lose sight or don't lose sight means I must think about it, I must attend it, attend it, attend it.

588
00:46:06,585 --> 00:46:07,085
[SPEAKER_00]: No, yeah.

589
00:46:07,245 --> 00:46:09,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Losing sight means being unclear about it.

590
00:46:10,305 --> 00:46:11,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just have to re-clarify.

591
00:46:11,786 --> 00:46:15,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's always something that I can't quite attend to.

592
00:46:15,447 --> 00:46:15,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, exactly.

593
00:46:15,927 --> 00:46:18,168
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always, it can only ever be.

594
00:46:18,588 --> 00:46:22,530
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the level of your sight-sight, which is on the level of your attention.

595
00:46:25,531 --> 00:46:36,956
[SPEAKER_02]: It's also like, it's different than having certain techniques, like,

596
00:46:38,580 --> 00:46:43,404
[SPEAKER_02]: It's trying to rebuild the context through emulating things that you would think be coming out of that context.

597
00:47:05,172 --> 00:47:07,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, a non-angry person would speak like this.

598
00:47:07,253 --> 00:47:09,194
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't know that because you're already angry.

599
00:47:09,914 --> 00:47:12,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a non-angry person would speak how?

600
00:47:12,295 --> 00:47:17,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why don't I remove my anger and then whichever way I speak will be a non-angry way of speaking.

601
00:47:17,677 --> 00:47:17,977
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

602
00:47:18,317 --> 00:47:25,560
[SPEAKER_02]: And others might be witness to you and then start writing down all the things that you do when you respond.

603
00:47:25,980 --> 00:47:26,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

604
00:47:26,735 --> 00:47:27,316
[SPEAKER_02]: He says this.

605
00:47:27,556 --> 00:47:28,697
[SPEAKER_02]: He asks the person that.

606
00:47:28,777 --> 00:47:30,338
[SPEAKER_02]: And create a new technique.

607
00:47:30,358 --> 00:47:31,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, called non-violence.

608
00:47:31,819 --> 00:47:32,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

609
00:47:32,840 --> 00:47:34,241
[SPEAKER_01]: They might even disapprove.

610
00:47:34,641 --> 00:47:37,163
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll say, no, that's not how a non-angry person would speak.

611
00:47:37,343 --> 00:47:37,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

612
00:47:37,803 --> 00:47:39,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that what's happened in the suttas with the arahant.

613
00:47:40,225 --> 00:47:40,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

614
00:47:40,645 --> 00:47:42,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was fully enlightened, but was upsetting people.

615
00:47:42,747 --> 00:47:45,068
[SPEAKER_00]: They say, I saw you got angry there, so you're obviously not going to lie.

616
00:47:45,088 --> 00:47:46,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, people just assumed that he was angry.

617
00:47:48,110 --> 00:47:50,872
[SPEAKER_01]: They just assumed he was angry because he was speaking in such a harsh manner.

618
00:47:52,053 --> 00:47:53,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they call him, send him to the Buddha.

619
00:47:53,574 --> 00:47:53,794
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha

620
00:47:55,186 --> 00:47:55,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Started talking to himself.

621
00:47:55,966 --> 00:47:56,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, no, he's an arahant.

622
00:47:57,726 --> 00:47:58,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

623
00:47:58,287 --> 00:48:00,207
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like how you're speaking.

624
00:48:01,087 --> 00:48:10,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And often, there are then, sort of, they're like... they're holy life companions in the holy life that are pleasing to listen, pleasing to sort of be with.

625
00:48:10,169 --> 00:48:18,050
[SPEAKER_01]: They're so friendly, so kind, so... so generous, so helpful with evil wishes, ignorant of the wrong view.

626
00:48:19,110 --> 00:48:21,611
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're the ones they're really hard to live with.

627
00:48:21,631 --> 00:48:22,771
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't speak pleasantly.

628
00:48:22,871 --> 00:48:24,011
[SPEAKER_01]: They're rough around the edges.

629
00:48:24,643 --> 00:48:25,723
[SPEAKER_01]: But they're of the right view.

630
00:48:28,264 --> 00:48:30,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the other ones, they're both.

631
00:48:30,105 --> 00:48:33,026
[SPEAKER_01]: But you... the Buddha clearly says which one's more important.

632
00:48:33,126 --> 00:48:33,626
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the view.

633
00:48:34,467 --> 00:48:35,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can... you can...

634
00:48:36,047 --> 00:48:39,709
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can refine your behavior to not upset vast amount of people, great.

635
00:48:39,869 --> 00:48:41,509
[SPEAKER_01]: But don't confuse that to be the practice.

636
00:48:42,710 --> 00:48:47,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't confuse that that will somehow regenerate the context that you have no knowledge of.

637
00:48:47,471 --> 00:48:48,252
[SPEAKER_01]: How can that happen?

638
00:48:49,072 --> 00:48:51,793
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no knowledge of the context of absence of ill-will.

639
00:48:52,561 --> 00:49:01,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, I'm hoping to regenerate it through reading the non-violent communication books and just sort of going through the motion of softly spoken, tailored words.

640
00:49:01,108 --> 00:49:04,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to learn how to look in a different direction.

641
00:49:04,351 --> 00:49:04,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

642
00:49:05,772 --> 00:49:07,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Yonis direction.

643
00:49:07,514 --> 00:49:11,777
[SPEAKER_00]: In a more general, in a more encompassing direction.

644
00:49:12,318 --> 00:49:14,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And which is just inquiring into your motivations.

645
00:49:15,120 --> 00:49:16,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the... that's the gateway.

646
00:49:17,722 --> 00:49:20,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why everything, as I said, where we started is converges on action.

647
00:49:21,723 --> 00:49:24,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Convergence of action is basically rooted in motivations.

648
00:49:25,185 --> 00:49:26,665
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's a good action or a bad action.

649
00:49:28,826 --> 00:49:30,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about, oh, I must stop doing.

650
00:49:30,607 --> 00:49:33,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, of course, you stop doing things that are against the precepts.

651
00:49:33,889 --> 00:49:37,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, people don't know where to draw a line, and then, whatever, oh, I must stop doing it.

652
00:49:37,491 --> 00:49:38,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that becomes your form of doing.

653
00:49:39,071 --> 00:49:42,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Abstaining from doing everything else, it's what I'm doing all the time, 24-7.

654
00:49:44,034 --> 00:49:46,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's a very frustrating position to be in.

655
00:49:48,442 --> 00:49:49,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize you can't stop the doing.

656
00:49:50,002 --> 00:49:52,663
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to stop the doing is to understand the roots of your action.

657
00:49:54,144 --> 00:49:58,445
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to replace the bad doing with good doing, and then get to understand it.

658
00:49:58,625 --> 00:50:01,646
[SPEAKER_01]: But just doing the good will not free you from the nature of action.

659
00:50:07,547 --> 00:50:08,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, doing the good.

660
00:50:10,628 --> 00:50:14,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Focusing like the... for example, like the non-violent communication technique.

661
00:50:16,120 --> 00:50:19,062
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's all coming... it's all coming based on anger.

662
00:50:19,683 --> 00:50:20,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

663
00:50:21,044 --> 00:50:24,746
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why your anger becomes implicit in your practice to be free from anger.

664
00:50:28,029 --> 00:50:28,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, I'm just...

665
00:50:29,250 --> 00:50:45,502
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if this isn't following on exactly what you just said, but from what we were saying before, if you are clear about your motivations, like fully clear, in which case there won't be any delusion in regard to

666
00:50:48,152 --> 00:50:52,433
[SPEAKER_00]: The context that you're in, then, in a sense, you don't need to worry about actions anymore.

667
00:50:52,453 --> 00:50:54,673
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no kind of... you don't need to sort of do anything.

668
00:50:54,753 --> 00:50:55,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

669
00:50:55,574 --> 00:50:58,594
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes effortless because it's absence of action.

670
00:50:58,754 --> 00:51:00,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, now that's the absence of action.

671
00:51:01,295 --> 00:51:03,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you also go beyond doubt as well.

672
00:51:04,235 --> 00:51:07,576
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're unclear about the motivation of your actions, you will be doubting it.

673
00:51:08,136 --> 00:51:10,957
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody starts questioning it, you'll start doubting it yourself.

674
00:51:11,677 --> 00:51:14,397
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't matter how much you doubt the content.

675
00:51:14,417 --> 00:51:16,898
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be affected by the doubt towards the content.

676
00:51:17,472 --> 00:51:24,477
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not doubtful in regard to the motivation that made you perform that content in the first place, so you will not be able to doubt it.

677
00:51:25,317 --> 00:51:27,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, maybe I could have done like this or this, but it doesn't matter.

678
00:51:27,578 --> 00:51:28,779
[SPEAKER_01]: I know why I've done it.

679
00:51:29,660 --> 00:51:34,323
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when people are unclear about that, that's when you just doubt this thing all over you.

680
00:51:34,783 --> 00:51:36,184
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's also, it's the end of action.

681
00:51:37,405 --> 00:51:38,445
[SPEAKER_00]: If fully developed, yes.

682
00:51:38,625 --> 00:51:39,366
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean, yeah.

683
00:51:39,386 --> 00:51:45,970
[SPEAKER_01]: In case you're not... Because... Because... Because it's just intentions giving rise to all these other intentional actions and that's it.

684
00:51:46,508 --> 00:51:47,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, there's nothing that needs to be...

685
00:51:47,708 --> 00:51:57,171
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no choice based on ignoring the context that's being made towards those actions, which is what the patujan situation is.

686
00:51:57,191 --> 00:52:06,814
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, because, like, yeah, because the patujan is always making individual choices at more... at various particular levels of particularity.

687
00:52:06,834 --> 00:52:09,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas, if it's all guarded right at the top...

688
00:52:09,935 --> 00:52:10,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

689
00:52:10,595 --> 00:52:13,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he's making choices because...

690
00:52:13,195 --> 00:52:13,676
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fine.

691
00:52:14,176 --> 00:52:14,996
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not aware...

692
00:52:15,564 --> 00:52:19,585
[SPEAKER_01]: that the choices can be made without needing him to be there making them.

693
00:52:21,086 --> 00:52:27,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's ignoring the general or rather he's unaware, has no clarity in regard to general context.

694
00:52:27,207 --> 00:52:27,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

695
00:52:27,888 --> 00:52:32,429
[SPEAKER_01]: That means he immediately implies then that he's the sole criteria for making decisions.

696
00:52:32,449 --> 00:52:32,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

697
00:52:33,489 --> 00:52:43,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that those decisions can be made only because they were given as... as... as simultaneously arisen options for you to attend and then choose on account of.

698
00:52:44,704 --> 00:52:45,504
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you ignore that.

699
00:52:45,865 --> 00:52:47,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, what else is there making choices?

700
00:52:48,005 --> 00:52:48,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's only me.

701
00:52:49,326 --> 00:52:49,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, I am.

702
00:52:50,527 --> 00:52:51,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, I must be.

703
00:52:52,347 --> 00:52:55,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, choices that he makes are choices rooted in his self-view.

704
00:52:55,569 --> 00:53:03,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you recognize that even I am making this choice, you realize, because in the general context that provided you with these options, you realize it's not rooted in you.

705
00:53:03,952 --> 00:53:10,415
[SPEAKER_01]: The roots of your choice are not rooted in you, which then means the actions that were rooted in your choice cannot be rooted in you either.

706
00:53:11,076 --> 00:53:14,097
[SPEAKER_02]: So, a Pitujana's context is

707
00:53:15,875 --> 00:53:17,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Me.

708
00:53:17,597 --> 00:53:26,028
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can easily see that when you ask yourself what's the context, you always find it as like... it's kind of not me.

709
00:53:26,168 --> 00:53:32,776
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's... the context is always prior to...

710
00:53:34,520 --> 00:53:39,384
[SPEAKER_00]: My own particular choices that I make, those choices... Well, that would be true only in case of an arahant.

711
00:53:40,024 --> 00:53:43,387
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to start from a neutral position, that answer is true.

712
00:53:43,727 --> 00:53:45,568
[SPEAKER_01]: But Puthujan is not in a neutral position.

713
00:53:45,688 --> 00:53:53,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order for him to arrive at that, that choice is already there, the only way to correctly arrive at it, is to first accept the choices first on him.

714
00:53:54,715 --> 00:53:57,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, this choice that's on me, is then on what?

715
00:53:58,238 --> 00:53:59,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence that acceptance of responsibility.

716
00:53:59,739 --> 00:54:00,720
[SPEAKER_00]: But can't you see that that

717
00:54:02,514 --> 00:54:10,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm responsible for these particular choices, but those choices are always made within a context that's always already given.

718
00:54:11,002 --> 00:54:11,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

719
00:54:11,662 --> 00:54:14,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you ask yourself the next question.

720
00:54:14,745 --> 00:54:18,789
[SPEAKER_01]: This seems to be clear to me, I seem to have understood it, and we're completely free from suffering.

721
00:54:19,369 --> 00:54:25,495
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is no, means you haven't fully understood it, there is still appropriation of action, means there is still absence of arahantship.

722
00:54:27,307 --> 00:54:34,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, I'm not denying that there are different degrees of understanding this, but you shouldn't stop short until you're completely beyond it.

723
00:54:36,991 --> 00:54:53,999
[SPEAKER_03]: So, since we're back on to sort of like where the... how this is done on a practical level, say that... see, like saying where your motivation is when it comes to an action,

724
00:54:56,440 --> 00:55:05,925
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to have a degree of awareness, wholesome and unwholesome, to say, this is right, this is... Yeah, yeah, sure.

725
00:55:05,965 --> 00:55:08,726
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, see, everybody has a degree of that awareness.

726
00:55:10,187 --> 00:55:11,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can then refine it even further.

727
00:55:14,369 --> 00:55:16,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody knows what's bad to do with your body.

728
00:55:16,910 --> 00:55:19,251
[SPEAKER_01]: To kill, to steal, to cheat, to lie, and so on.

729
00:55:19,411 --> 00:55:20,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everybody knows that.

730
00:55:22,873 --> 00:55:24,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly, everyone who is practicing the dhamma.

731
00:55:25,425 --> 00:55:30,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, sure, without compromising on that principle, you can start refining it further.

732
00:55:30,927 --> 00:55:40,069
[SPEAKER_00]: But, doesn't the Buddha say, you know, samādhi, citta, only the harious advocate knows the whole thing?

733
00:55:40,109 --> 00:55:42,470
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, he understands the roots of it, sure.

734
00:55:42,490 --> 00:55:45,230
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't mean the puṭṭhujana has no knowledge of what virtue is.

735
00:55:45,590 --> 00:55:48,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he has some sense of something being right, something wrong, but he hasn't actually got the proper...

736
00:55:48,871 --> 00:55:49,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, virtue.

737
00:55:49,211 --> 00:55:50,092
[SPEAKER_01]: He has a sense of virtue.

738
00:55:52,772 --> 00:55:53,072
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

739
00:55:56,010 --> 00:56:16,895
[SPEAKER_03]: The point I was sort of, like, I was getting at is that, like, even for the sense of virtue, like, even if you say you have doubts about sense of virtue to a certain degree of some aspect of it or another, and, like, in order to develop that, you sort of start with, eh, not sure what the exact translation is, hīryo tattva.

740
00:56:17,475 --> 00:56:17,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

741
00:56:18,915 --> 00:56:21,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that usually translates as sense of shame and fear of wrongdoing.

742
00:56:21,776 --> 00:56:21,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

743
00:56:23,147 --> 00:56:31,129
[SPEAKER_03]: So, fear of wrongdoing and sense of shame already implies a degree of action, isn't it?

744
00:56:31,830 --> 00:56:34,670
[SPEAKER_03]: Like... Like... What do you mean?

745
00:56:34,910 --> 00:56:48,654
[SPEAKER_03]: Like... See, like, it implies virtue, as in, like, a sense of virtue, as in, you know... Well, it implies fear of unwholesome.

746
00:56:49,095 --> 00:56:50,375
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but...

747
00:56:51,068 --> 00:57:18,881
[SPEAKER_03]: The practicality of it is that the fear of wrongdoing, it's sort of like you have a certain awareness of where your action is rooted.

748
00:57:22,048 --> 00:57:22,528
[SPEAKER_03]: What action?

749
00:57:23,869 --> 00:57:28,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Like any action, like... Well, it's a... it's a context for your actions.

750
00:57:29,290 --> 00:57:33,151
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a context for abstaining from wrong actions.

751
00:57:33,251 --> 00:57:39,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing fear of wrongdoing means in developing context of not acting unwholesomely, because you have fear of it.

752
00:57:41,673 --> 00:57:45,614
[SPEAKER_01]: So, out of the fear of wrongdoing, then your actions won't be as unwholesome.

753
00:57:47,775 --> 00:57:47,975
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

754
00:57:48,947 --> 00:57:53,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a context for your actions and for absence of particular other unwholesome actions.

755
00:57:54,570 --> 00:57:58,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be abstaining from unwholesome actions if you don't have fear of wrongdoing.

756
00:57:59,832 --> 00:58:00,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You would just be doing it.

757
00:58:01,533 --> 00:58:02,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you not doing it then?

758
00:58:02,773 --> 00:58:04,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you practicing restraint?

759
00:58:05,734 --> 00:58:08,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, because you develop fear of non-restraint.

760
00:58:09,196 --> 00:58:12,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you recognize that to be the basis for the right practice.

761
00:58:14,718 --> 00:58:16,139
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, like, why do I want to practice?

762
00:58:16,279 --> 00:58:18,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, because I want to be free from suffering and so on.

763
00:58:22,272 --> 00:58:23,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's why it's useful.

764
00:58:24,913 --> 00:58:26,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like go around neurotically and fear everything.

765
00:58:26,914 --> 00:58:27,494
[SPEAKER_01]: What have I done?

766
00:58:28,034 --> 00:58:28,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Was this wholesome?

767
00:58:28,695 --> 00:58:29,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Was this unwholesome?

768
00:58:30,075 --> 00:58:30,195
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

769
00:58:30,215 --> 00:58:36,518
[SPEAKER_01]: It's recognizing that things rooted in lust, aversion, distraction are unwholesome.

770
00:58:36,538 --> 00:58:42,420
[SPEAKER_01]: And you value absence of those unwholesome things so much that you fear the possibility of their presence.

771
00:58:43,400 --> 00:58:44,981
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're afraid of their presence,

772
00:58:46,764 --> 00:58:48,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be impossible for you to act towards them.

773
00:58:49,705 --> 00:58:55,728
[SPEAKER_01]: If you lose the fear of their possibility, then, yeah, you start getting closer and you start acting towards it.

774
00:58:57,008 --> 00:58:59,349
[SPEAKER_03]: And to refine your awareness of your actions as well.

775
00:58:59,369 --> 00:58:59,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

776
00:58:59,970 --> 00:59:03,731
[SPEAKER_01]: To basically refine your abstinence from unwholesome actions.

777
00:59:04,331 --> 00:59:13,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And then abstinence of unwholesome actions means abstinence from perpetuation of unwholesome principles of craving, lust and so on.

778
00:59:14,604 --> 00:59:17,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And now that you're not perpetuating them, they start fading.

779
00:59:17,545 --> 00:59:21,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And once they sort of thin out, you can see through them much better.

780
00:59:23,808 --> 00:59:24,449
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's another thing.

781
00:59:24,469 --> 00:59:29,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there's something... Say there's like a mad rabbit dog on the other side of the gate.

782
00:59:30,832 --> 00:59:35,154
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you know the risks, the implications of that kind of animal.

783
00:59:35,774 --> 00:59:36,334
[SPEAKER_01]: You fear it.

784
00:59:36,475 --> 00:59:37,315
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't go near it.

785
00:59:37,775 --> 00:59:39,396
[SPEAKER_01]: You make sure the gate is boarded.

786
00:59:41,216 --> 00:59:41,676
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

787
00:59:41,956 --> 00:59:45,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You've done your bit and you feel safe where you are.

788
00:59:47,818 --> 00:59:50,999
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have a fear of that which is on the other side in case you get there.

789
00:59:51,199 --> 00:59:53,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So you make sure you don't get there.

790
00:59:53,440 --> 00:59:58,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But I imagine you don't have that context of that fearful animal.

791
00:59:58,921 --> 01:00:01,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm quite curious about this dog.

792
01:00:01,782 --> 01:00:02,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me go and see.

793
01:00:04,823 --> 01:00:08,544
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you make all these actions that result exposing you to that thing.

794
01:00:09,383 --> 01:00:13,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you could have prevented if you develop fear properly, in regard to a proper thing.

795
01:00:17,390 --> 01:00:24,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, the the way we encourage monks to sort of contemplate sensuality heights, provides little satisfaction, much greater danger.

796
01:00:25,717 --> 01:00:32,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if a presence of sensual object is seen implicitly dangerous,

797
01:00:34,322 --> 01:00:37,223
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't have to... oh, now I have to pull my mind back from it.

798
01:00:37,263 --> 01:00:37,403
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

799
01:00:37,463 --> 01:00:41,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind already won't be there, because it's dangerous.

800
01:00:41,785 --> 01:00:49,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Lacking the perceptions of danger, yeah, the mind will start detaining it.

801
01:00:49,408 --> 01:00:53,749
[SPEAKER_02]: But it causes a sense of shame, fear of wrongdoing.

802
01:00:54,450 --> 01:00:58,291
[SPEAKER_02]: It kind of signals that you're going in the direction...

803
01:00:58,311 --> 01:00:59,011
[SPEAKER_02]: They go very far.

804
01:00:59,372 --> 01:01:02,353
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is going against what you are trying to maintain.

805
01:01:02,753 --> 01:01:03,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

806
01:01:03,600 --> 01:01:13,989
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm trying to maintain a mind of loving-kindness, and now I'm going to go and beat someone up.

807
01:01:14,049 --> 01:01:19,974
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel uneasy, I feel shameful, as the signal going off, that you're going against that.

808
01:01:20,114 --> 01:01:26,359
[SPEAKER_01]: That signal will be more powerful when there is a greater discrepancy that you were about to create with your action.

809
01:01:26,539 --> 01:01:28,521
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the closer you get, the closer it gets.

810
01:01:28,541 --> 01:01:30,803
[SPEAKER_01]: So, somebody who's been practicing sense-restraint,

811
01:01:31,322 --> 01:01:33,923
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you also get to increase hīrīyānottapa.

812
01:01:34,344 --> 01:01:38,506
[SPEAKER_01]: You start valuing absence of sensuality more, and the more you do it, the more you value it.

813
01:01:39,126 --> 01:01:49,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, the prospect of doing it requires even greater discrepancy of your action to reach that further away, and which means then that the alarm will be much higher.

814
01:01:50,433 --> 01:01:58,017
[SPEAKER_01]: As if somebody who just yesterday decided to give it up, tomorrow he might feel a bit bad, but he doesn't feel the full weight of the danger of giving back into those things.

815
01:02:00,127 --> 01:02:01,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a buffer zone.

816
01:02:02,247 --> 01:02:02,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

817
01:02:02,728 --> 01:02:02,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

818
01:02:03,008 --> 01:02:03,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

819
01:02:03,288 --> 01:02:03,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

820
01:02:03,528 --> 01:02:03,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

821
01:02:03,788 --> 01:02:03,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

822
01:02:03,988 --> 01:02:04,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Alarm!

823
01:02:04,968 --> 01:02:11,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you practice, the bigger it is, which means the less likely is that you're going to cross it without becoming aware of what you're about to do.

824
01:02:11,669 --> 01:02:16,470
[SPEAKER_02]: It's what they call the guardians, isn't it?

825
01:02:16,510 --> 01:02:17,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the guardians of the world.

826
01:02:18,270 --> 01:02:18,490
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

827
01:02:19,731 --> 01:02:24,832
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, you become more sensitive to... you feel shame in taking sugar.

828
01:02:26,232 --> 01:02:26,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

829
01:02:26,988 --> 01:02:30,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the danger in the slightest fault, as the suttas would say.

830
01:02:30,292 --> 01:02:32,534
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you become neurotic.

831
01:02:34,436 --> 01:02:36,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it might happen as a side effect, but you can grow out of that.

832
01:02:37,600 --> 01:02:39,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But it means actually maintaining that context.

833
01:02:40,243 --> 01:02:43,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Even any significant thing, such as taking extra sugar, that's offered.

834
01:02:43,867 --> 01:02:45,008
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not breaking any rules.

835
01:02:45,629 --> 01:02:46,550
[SPEAKER_01]: But why?

836
01:02:47,580 --> 01:02:53,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I just have pure craving, desire for sweetness, sweet taste.

837
01:02:53,382 --> 01:02:57,904
[SPEAKER_01]: No physical purpose, no physical necessity or benefit, nutritional benefit.

838
01:02:58,264 --> 01:02:59,684
[SPEAKER_01]: I simply want this taste.

839
01:03:01,765 --> 01:03:06,666
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you use that as a criteria to not act, then you would be ashamed when you're about to act.

840
01:03:07,647 --> 01:03:10,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what it means, like, seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

841
01:03:10,608 --> 01:03:16,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, well, look, look, look, see, this room is this, and you folding a sitting cloth, that's an offense, that's an offense, see, that's a fault.

842
01:03:18,394 --> 01:03:19,975
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means recognizing where the faults are.

843
01:03:20,195 --> 01:03:22,056
[SPEAKER_01]: When things are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

844
01:03:22,817 --> 01:03:27,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if on paper, the vinaya says, yes, you can have all the sugar you want, because it's offered.

845
01:03:28,720 --> 01:03:30,921
[SPEAKER_01]: You will still feel ashamed, because you don't need it.

846
01:03:31,122 --> 01:03:34,183
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, you take it for pure sensual satisfaction.

847
01:03:34,263 --> 01:03:34,624
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

848
01:03:35,424 --> 01:03:38,826
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, from that attitude, that point of view, there are no loopholes in vinaya.

849
01:03:40,387 --> 01:03:41,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there are no loopholes to craving.

850
01:03:43,220 --> 01:03:49,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But if people just follow the letter of the rule, then the millions of rūpas... Oh, well, see, the Buddha didn't specify this, means I can do it.

851
01:03:51,342 --> 01:03:58,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you can do it or not, should not be rooted in whether it was written or said by the Buddha, should be rooted... Is it rooted in my greed, aversion, delusion?

852
01:03:59,984 --> 01:04:01,145
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, then you shouldn't be doing it.

853
01:04:01,165 --> 01:04:01,925
[SPEAKER_02]: New rule.

854
01:04:01,965 --> 01:04:03,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Here we go.

855
01:04:03,225 --> 01:04:03,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Just make that rule.

856
01:04:04,005 --> 01:04:04,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

857
01:04:04,525 --> 01:04:06,206
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, well, again, what Buddha said there is this...

858
01:04:06,226 --> 01:04:08,266
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, oh, I can't keep all these hundred-something rules.

859
01:04:08,326 --> 01:04:09,367
[SPEAKER_01]: The monk comes to the Buddha and says

860
01:04:11,415 --> 01:04:13,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do anything unwholesome by body, by speech, by mind.

861
01:04:13,596 --> 01:04:16,297
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not necessarily sort of a vinaya issue, is it?

862
01:04:16,317 --> 01:04:17,298
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just more...

863
01:04:17,818 --> 01:04:18,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

864
01:04:18,258 --> 01:04:26,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have no context whatsoever, you need this formulated vinaya to create that absolute outer edges of the boundary.

865
01:04:26,662 --> 01:04:28,923
[SPEAKER_01]: By you taking that to be the goal in itself,

866
01:04:29,438 --> 01:04:33,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you never actually get to understand where those actions are rooted and where the problem is.

867
01:04:34,164 --> 01:04:36,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically just like rounding up the symptoms.

868
01:04:36,427 --> 01:04:41,514
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you can't... like you can't spend your whole life being extremely strict and keeping vinaya.

869
01:04:41,534 --> 01:04:41,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

870
01:04:41,774 --> 01:04:42,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

871
01:04:42,094 --> 01:04:44,077
[SPEAKER_00]: But not be paying any attention to...

872
01:04:44,564 --> 01:04:46,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether what I'm doing is wholesome or unwholesome.

873
01:04:46,325 --> 01:04:49,346
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, so... And that's how you're affected by your own vinaya.

874
01:04:49,386 --> 01:04:52,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, it can still protect you to an extent.

875
01:04:52,847 --> 01:04:54,647
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still better than being unrestrained.

876
01:04:54,767 --> 01:04:55,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

877
01:04:55,267 --> 01:04:57,848
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, you need to make that step.

878
01:04:57,908 --> 01:04:58,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's true.

879
01:04:58,328 --> 01:04:59,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Like training wheels.

880
01:04:59,569 --> 01:05:01,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

881
01:05:01,609 --> 01:05:02,510
[SPEAKER_00]: In a boundary, I think.

882
01:05:02,530 --> 01:05:03,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

883
01:05:03,970 --> 01:05:07,051
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's why you can go beyond sīla.

884
01:05:07,091 --> 01:05:09,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting unwholesomely is not...

885
01:05:12,141 --> 01:05:16,982
[SPEAKER_01]: sīla, your virtue and sensory strain, does not prevent the root of unwholesome.

886
01:05:17,002 --> 01:05:19,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It just prevents the expression of it.

887
01:05:20,743 --> 01:05:29,965
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like you got sick, you caught some bacteria, that now you have, I don't know, fever as a result of it, and all these other symptoms.

888
01:05:30,025 --> 01:05:33,606
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, on account of those symptoms, you keep aggravating your situation further.

889
01:05:33,626 --> 01:05:35,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You go and jump in a cold pool,

890
01:05:36,111 --> 01:05:39,353
[SPEAKER_01]: To cool down your fever, which actually makes your bacteria even worse.

891
01:05:39,713 --> 01:05:40,534
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't know that.

892
01:05:40,574 --> 01:05:42,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You just deal what you see in front of you.

893
01:05:43,216 --> 01:05:49,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first thing you have to do is stop jumping in the cold pool that will aggravate your fever, that will help your fever, but aggravate the cause of the fever.

894
01:05:49,680 --> 01:05:51,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start practicing restraint.

895
01:05:52,782 --> 01:05:53,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Control the symptoms.

896
01:05:53,662 --> 01:06:05,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, if you think that all you have to do is abstain from going out into the cold water, because it won't aggravate the fever, and you think that would be enough to then uproot the root cause of the fever as well, that's not going to happen.

897
01:06:08,362 --> 01:06:15,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, vinaya and sīla is that outer edges who basically, okay, don't aggravate the aggravated symptoms anymore.

898
01:06:16,086 --> 01:06:16,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop here.

899
01:06:17,227 --> 01:06:20,729
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you think that stopping there will also then clear you of the symptoms, that won't happen.

900
01:06:21,469 --> 01:06:23,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Only uprooting the cause will then remove the symptoms.

901
01:06:26,332 --> 01:06:26,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Same like the...

902
01:06:27,653 --> 01:06:29,634
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know why I'm complicating with a different simile.

903
01:06:29,814 --> 01:06:32,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Simile of the itch, the wounds on your skin that they would get.

904
01:06:34,380 --> 01:06:35,701
[SPEAKER_01]: First thing, you have to stop scratching.

905
01:06:35,781 --> 01:06:38,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Scratching will not uproot the bacterial infection.

906
01:06:39,604 --> 01:06:45,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, eh... cessation of scratching will not uproot the bacterial infection, but it will certainly stop spreading it.

907
01:06:46,329 --> 01:06:50,992
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can apply medicine for the underlying cause of that which made you itch in the first place.

908
01:06:52,933 --> 01:06:54,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically the vinaya.

909
01:06:54,334 --> 01:06:56,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Stops you from making things worse.

910
01:06:57,376 --> 01:07:01,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But taking that to be a practice for uprooting it, well, that's extremely wrong view.

911
01:07:01,900 --> 01:07:02,800
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a first fetter.

912
01:07:04,472 --> 01:07:09,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And unless you abandon it, you won't get the right view, let alone free yourself from lust and aversion later on.

913
01:07:12,918 --> 01:07:19,902
[SPEAKER_03]: Say, if you're confused about where my action is, you can always know in regards to the virtue, as in, I'm not doing this.

914
01:07:20,202 --> 01:07:20,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

915
01:07:20,582 --> 01:07:23,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can be confused in terms of, on a more particular level.

916
01:07:23,304 --> 01:07:24,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, is this resistant?

917
01:07:24,225 --> 01:07:27,207
[SPEAKER_01]: But, okay, overall, is this wholesome or unwholesome?

918
01:07:27,988 --> 01:07:30,188
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not unwholesome, then okay.

919
01:07:30,568 --> 01:07:32,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait and you might get clarity in regard... like that.

920
01:07:33,129 --> 01:07:34,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I taking... like, there's nothing wrong.

921
01:07:34,649 --> 01:07:35,869
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not stealing this sugar.

922
01:07:36,890 --> 01:07:43,811
[SPEAKER_01]: But am I taking it because I really need it, because I really, like, didn't have enough calories, or I'm taking it purely for pleasure's sake?

923
01:07:44,491 --> 01:07:46,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as I said, if you're unsure, then don't do it.

924
01:07:49,052 --> 01:07:49,812
[SPEAKER_01]: As a rule of thumb.

925
01:07:51,412 --> 01:07:52,812
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if what I'm about to do...

926
01:07:52,912 --> 01:07:56,113
[SPEAKER_01]: I know it's not unwholesome to the level of breaking rules and so on,

927
01:07:56,865 --> 01:08:03,350
[SPEAKER_01]: But on a more personal level, on a more immediate level, I'm not sure whether it's desire or without desire.

928
01:08:03,390 --> 01:08:04,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, don't do it.

929
01:08:06,132 --> 01:08:10,376
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a disagreeable experience in regard to it, means desire was involved.

930
01:08:10,476 --> 01:08:13,738
[SPEAKER_01]: If you generally then don't care, means there was no desire involved.

931
01:08:14,739 --> 01:08:20,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you wouldn't be affected by saying no to things, unless you had like a desirous mind towards them.

932
01:08:22,345 --> 01:08:24,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're doubting, it's most likely rooted in desire.

933
01:08:26,012 --> 01:08:27,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, doubt is an unwholesome thing.

934
01:08:28,193 --> 01:08:29,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in the sense, I must stop doubting.

935
01:08:29,833 --> 01:08:30,433
[SPEAKER_01]: I must stop thinking.

936
01:08:30,453 --> 01:08:30,593
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

937
01:08:31,013 --> 01:08:35,695
[SPEAKER_01]: I must clarify my mind to go beyond this doubt, so the doubt cannot enter it.

938
01:08:36,435 --> 01:08:37,555
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't control the doubt.

939
01:08:37,795 --> 01:08:39,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Doubt is another symptom.

940
01:08:39,996 --> 01:08:41,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in lack of clarity.

941
01:08:43,137 --> 01:08:48,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say clarity, it means lack of... lack of clarity means lack of understanding, not lack of information.

942
01:08:50,187 --> 01:08:54,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sometimes people... yeah, sometimes you don't know enough, so you read the rules and so on, but sometimes people, that's all they do.

943
01:08:55,108 --> 01:09:00,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Just need to keep re-reading and be told the same thing, on and on and on and on, to help them deal with the doubt.

944
01:09:00,149 --> 01:09:02,430
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not dealing with the doubt, that's just feeding it further.

945
01:09:03,190 --> 01:09:08,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You want external authority to remove the doubt for you, because you're not seeing for yourself where your actions are rooted.

946
01:09:11,952 --> 01:09:15,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why practically you can check that general context, what you're about to do.

947
01:09:15,933 --> 01:09:17,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it clearly unwholesome?

948
01:09:17,514 --> 01:09:18,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it's non-negotiable.

949
01:09:19,140 --> 01:09:20,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want to go for a walk.

950
01:09:20,181 --> 01:09:20,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

951
01:09:20,381 --> 01:09:21,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because I'm restless?

952
01:09:21,682 --> 01:09:22,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Or because I need some exercise?

953
01:09:24,544 --> 01:09:26,846
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're unclear, well, then perhaps wait.

954
01:09:29,428 --> 01:09:30,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Decide, no, I won't go for a walk.

955
01:09:31,650 --> 01:09:34,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I feel affected now, emotionally, if I have to stay here?

956
01:09:34,192 --> 01:09:34,352
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

957
01:09:34,652 --> 01:09:37,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, okay, so then I probably wasn't trying to resist any restlessness.

958
01:09:37,834 --> 01:09:39,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can go for a walk.

959
01:09:39,936 --> 01:09:42,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the only method you can really do.

960
01:09:42,818 --> 01:09:44,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Just keep questioning whatever you're about to do.

961
01:09:45,944 --> 01:09:46,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, is this right or wrong?"

962
01:09:46,965 --> 01:09:47,565
[SPEAKER_01]: or something like that.

963
01:09:47,585 --> 01:09:47,645
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

964
01:09:47,665 --> 01:09:49,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, where is it rooted?

965
01:09:50,467 --> 01:09:53,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Right or wrong has been sort of dealt with on the level of vinaya.

966
01:09:54,569 --> 01:09:55,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, it's on a practical level.

967
01:09:55,910 --> 01:09:56,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is it rooted?

968
01:09:56,610 --> 01:10:03,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the same thing, externally, can sometimes be wholesome, sometimes unwholesome, depending on whether it's rooted in your desire or not.

969
01:10:05,395 --> 01:10:08,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's what we were starting with, which is like asking... That's about the same, yeah.

970
01:10:09,157 --> 01:10:09,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing this?

971
01:10:09,978 --> 01:10:10,958
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my motivation?

972
01:10:11,258 --> 01:10:14,080
[SPEAKER_00]: And not expecting to have the answer immediately.

973
01:10:14,858 --> 01:10:15,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

974
01:10:15,939 --> 01:10:17,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Discerning it, what's what's already there enduring.

975
01:10:17,879 --> 01:10:19,860
[SPEAKER_01]: If I don't... oh, yeah, it's because of this.

976
01:10:19,940 --> 01:10:20,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, fine.

977
01:10:20,661 --> 01:10:21,961
[SPEAKER_01]: But why am I doing it right now?

978
01:10:23,442 --> 01:10:23,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Where?

979
01:10:24,082 --> 01:10:29,124
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it that motivates me to go and want to go for a walk?

980
01:10:29,144 --> 01:10:30,285
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no particular reason.

981
01:10:30,465 --> 01:10:30,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

982
01:10:31,565 --> 01:10:31,946
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

983
01:10:32,006 --> 01:10:32,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

984
01:10:32,446 --> 01:10:33,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Answer that presented itself.

985
01:10:33,986 --> 01:10:35,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have no particular reason.

986
01:10:35,067 --> 01:10:42,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that non-particular reason, is it possible that's rooted maybe in me feeling uncomfortable in the kuti on my own?

987
01:10:43,462 --> 01:10:44,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Start investigating, of course.

988
01:10:44,903 --> 01:10:46,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it going to be unwholesome?

989
01:10:46,284 --> 01:10:46,905
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not clear.

990
01:10:47,125 --> 01:10:47,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

991
01:10:48,126 --> 01:10:48,966
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the starting point.

992
01:10:49,427 --> 01:10:52,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this lack of clarity rooted in unwholesome?

993
01:10:53,830 --> 01:10:56,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when you realize that you really don't mind, even if somebody says, you know, you can't go for a walk.

994
01:10:57,093 --> 01:11:01,396
[SPEAKER_01]: If you decide not to go for a walk and you're not going to be affected if you have to stay in the kuti, then it's okay.

995
01:11:01,417 --> 01:11:02,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, maybe it wasn't rooted in that, then.

996
01:11:03,478 --> 01:11:04,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, you know it wasn't rooted in that.

997
01:11:04,999 --> 01:11:05,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you don't need to find...

998
01:11:06,160 --> 01:11:08,042
[SPEAKER_01]: I must find where exactly it's rooted.

999
01:11:08,940 --> 01:11:09,060
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1000
01:11:09,180 --> 01:11:11,782
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to find is that it's not rooted in unwholesome.

1001
01:11:12,383 --> 01:11:16,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else will be fine.

1002
01:11:21,851 --> 01:11:24,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will also avoid psychologizing.

1003
01:11:24,893 --> 01:11:28,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Because psychologizing will be focusing on providing the content, the reason.

1004
01:11:29,497 --> 01:11:30,918
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't... you don't need a reason.

1005
01:11:31,679 --> 01:11:37,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to make sure that the absence of these reasons are not caused by greed, aversion, delusion.

1006
01:11:40,037 --> 01:11:40,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

1007
01:11:52,906 --> 01:11:56,829
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's where we began this interrogation.

1008
01:11:56,869 --> 01:11:59,191
[SPEAKER_02]: The importance of interrogation.

1009
01:11:59,671 --> 01:11:59,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1010
01:12:00,351 --> 01:12:09,418
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... like this, of course, it's basically... well, we recorded it, so you get like a reference collected, so people can listen, including ourselves.

1011
01:12:10,095 --> 01:12:11,436
[SPEAKER_01]: You listen to it and, ah, okay, yeah.

1012
01:12:11,816 --> 01:12:13,076
[SPEAKER_01]: It reminds you how to do it.

1013
01:12:13,997 --> 01:12:16,398
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a practice you should be doing to yourself all the time.

1014
01:12:17,158 --> 01:12:26,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, when you go, or everyone goes to their own kuti on their own in the afternoon, and sort of, you're about to do something, you... Oh, is this awesome?

1015
01:12:26,542 --> 01:12:28,403
[SPEAKER_01]: No, just, okay, I'm about to do this.

1016
01:12:28,843 --> 01:12:29,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

1017
01:12:30,224 --> 01:12:30,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I doing that?

1018
01:12:30,984 --> 01:12:34,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, are there any red flags coming up, shame?

1019
01:12:38,196 --> 01:12:46,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, just checking peripherally if there are red flags, not necessarily trying to interfere or control what you're about to do.

1020
01:12:46,441 --> 01:12:53,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, say you go back to Kuti now, you finish eating and then, I want to go for a walk.

1021
01:12:55,846 --> 01:12:56,847
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no problem with that.

1022
01:12:57,767 --> 01:13:00,029
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you should do it immediately by default.

1023
01:13:00,189 --> 01:13:01,370
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you ask yourself, why?

1024
01:13:01,470 --> 01:13:02,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is this action rooted?

1025
01:13:03,471 --> 01:13:05,452
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not looking for faults, I'm just looking for clarity.

1026
01:13:06,494 --> 01:13:10,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's rooted in the fact that I'm getting quite sleepy, and I don't want to sleep.

1027
01:13:11,018 --> 01:13:18,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Going out in a cold air, in the wind, walking a bit, will certainly wake me up, or at least has a greater chance of waking me up, than if I don't do it.

1028
01:13:18,746 --> 01:13:19,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you do it.

1029
01:13:19,426 --> 01:13:20,688
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing unwholesome about it.

1030
01:13:23,090 --> 01:13:30,357
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's purely unwholesome reason, because I heard there are people on the road down there, I heard voices, and I just want to see who there was, and maybe talk to people.

1031
01:13:32,529 --> 01:13:35,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And then that becomes clearly, oh, that's why you want to do it.

1032
01:13:35,110 --> 01:13:38,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that's what you're leaning to, that's what you're hoping for.

1033
01:13:38,331 --> 01:13:40,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're unsure, oh, is it or not?

1034
01:13:40,552 --> 01:13:44,094
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I were to go walking in the opposite direction, when I know there is nobody?

1035
01:13:44,774 --> 01:13:46,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I feel dejected on account of it?

1036
01:13:47,555 --> 01:13:48,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, yes.

1037
01:13:48,616 --> 01:13:53,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I had the desire, basically, to go down to talk to people on the pretext of having a healthy walk.

1038
01:13:55,098 --> 01:14:00,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's nothing like, oh, how do you... You just need to ask yourself and then look and wait for the answer.

1039
01:14:00,701 --> 01:14:01,061
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there.

1040
01:14:01,632 --> 01:14:06,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, you wouldn't have had that thought of going for a walk where the people are, if it wasn't rooted in that motivation.

1041
01:14:09,914 --> 01:14:13,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not... it's... it... Nobody can say, oh, that's beyond me.

1042
01:14:13,796 --> 01:14:16,877
[SPEAKER_01]: This practice is beyond me.

1043
01:14:16,977 --> 01:14:22,398
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's truly beyond you, means you're incapable of act, acting and intending.

1044
01:14:22,498 --> 01:14:23,819
[SPEAKER_01]: And nobody's incapable of that.

1045
01:14:24,419 --> 01:14:28,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means everybody's incapable of being aware of the motivation of those acts.

1046
01:14:31,555 --> 01:14:34,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's unwholesome, don't do it.

1047
01:14:36,398 --> 01:14:37,399
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's wholesome, do it.

1048
01:14:37,619 --> 01:14:38,679
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you have to do.

1049
01:14:39,800 --> 01:14:41,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we've said millions of times.

1050
01:14:42,402 --> 01:14:44,243
[SPEAKER_01]: That which you know it's unskillful, abandon it.

1051
01:14:44,723 --> 01:14:46,124
[SPEAKER_01]: That which you know it's skillful, cultivate it.

1052
01:14:46,764 --> 01:14:47,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is skillful, unskillful?

1053
01:14:48,005 --> 01:14:50,707
[SPEAKER_01]: It's whether it's rooted in greed, aversion, delusion in your mind, or not.

1054
01:14:51,387 --> 01:14:51,748
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

1055
01:14:53,469 --> 01:14:54,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Interrogation.

1056
01:14:54,309 --> 01:14:55,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I must do this.

1057
01:14:55,330 --> 01:14:56,430
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, and then I practice.

1058
01:14:56,450 --> 01:14:56,550
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1059
01:14:56,611 --> 01:14:57,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your practice.

1060
01:14:58,872 --> 01:15:00,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Uprooting any, eh...

1061
01:15:01,149 --> 01:15:05,773
[SPEAKER_01]: uprooting the maintenance of unskillful states of mind that are maintained through the unskillful actions.

1062
01:15:07,375 --> 01:15:13,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't have unskillful action being performed without the unskillful intention in the mind persisting with it.

1063
01:15:14,481 --> 01:15:21,748
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can certainly uproot the unskillful intention in your mind by abstaining from performing unskillful actions with your body and your speech.

